// Practice quiz: Password hashing
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/api-security/password-hashing

::o1 match::How do you check a login attempt against a stored delegating hash? {
=encoder.matches(raw, stored)#Correct. It handles the salt and algorithm.
~raw.equals(stored)#Not quite. The stored value is a hash, not the password.
~encoder.encode(raw).equals(stored)#Not quite. A new salt makes that comparison fail.
~decrypt the hash#Not quite. A hash cannot be decrypted.
}

::o2 why slow::Why should a password hash be slow? {
=It limits how many guesses an attacker can try#Correct. Login is rare, but attacker guessing is massive.
~It reduces database size#Not quite. It has no such effect.
~Login needs to feel secure#Not quite. Security is not a feeling.
~Fast hashes are not valid in Java#Not quite. They are valid, just unsuitable.
}

::o2 salt::What does a per-user salt defeat? {
=Precomputed tables, and identical hashes for identical passwords#Correct. Each hash must be attacked separately.
~Logging#Not quite. It is unrelated.
~Network sniffing#Not quite. That is a TLS matter.
~Weak passwords entirely#Not quite. Weak passwords are still easier to guess.
}

::o2 not encryption::Why not encrypt passwords instead of hashing them? {
=Encryption is reversible, so a leaked key exposes every password#Correct. You never need the original value.
~Encryption is always slower#Not quite. That is not the reason.
~Java has no encryption#Not quite. It does.
~Encrypted data cannot be stored#Not quite. It can.
}

::o3 prefix::What does the \{bcrypt\} prefix on a stored hash do? {
=Tells the delegating encoder which algorithm verifies it#Correct. That allows changing the default later.
~Encrypts the hash#Not quite. It is a label.
~Makes the hash longer for security#Not quite. The prefix adds no security.
~Names the user#Not quite. It names the algorithm.
}

